    {"issue": "The closing sentence of 2.1.f permits campus restriction or emergency suspension of \"any student involved in such an incident\" pending investigation, which cannot be squared with Section 22.2's rule that a student's status shall not be altered pending final action except for \"imminent danger.\" The two set different triggers for the same interim measure.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.28}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c007", "issues": [
    {"issue": "\"If both parties do not consent\" is ambiguous between \"if either party does not consent\" and \"if neither consents,\" and it presumes exactly two participants. The later edition of the same handbook replaces it with \"all parties,\" confirming the phrase was read as defective.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.40},
    {"issue": "Section 3.0 as written channels sexual misconduct allegations into the ordinary conduct machinery without saying so, while the 2013–2014 handbook's Section 3.0 expressly directs such allegations to the Special Examiner's Process in Section 22.6 \"and not the Student Conduct Process found in Section 19.\" Which process a student is owed for conduct spanning both editions is left unresolved.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.32}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c008", "issues": [
    {"issue": "\"Sexual contact\" is nowhere defined and \"explicit consent\" is qualified only by \"may be considered sexual misconduct,\" so the provision states no determinate rule of prohibited conduct. The gap between \"explicit\" consent and consent that is merely \"clearly communicated\" compounds the uncertainty.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.55},
    {"issue": "3.1's requirement of \"explicit consent\" cannot be squared with 3.3, which allows consent to be \"communicated verbally or through actions\" and lack of consent to be \"inferred.\" One provision demands an express manifestation; the other contemplates inference from conduct.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.33}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c009", "issues": [
    {"issue": "3.2 makes \"taking advantage of someone's incapacitation or intoxication\" misconduct, but 3.1 provides that only incapacity — not intoxication — destroys the ability to consent. Read together, 3.2 sanctions sexual activity with a person who under 3.1 is capable of consenting, and \"taking advantage\" is given no content.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.32}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c010", "issues": [
    {"issue": "3.3's allowance that consent \"may be communicated verbally or through actions\" and inferred from circumstances cannot be squared with 3.1's requirement of \"explicit consent\" for each act of sexual contact. A fact-finder applying both has no rule for consent manifested by conduct in an ongoing relationship.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.33}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c011", "issues": [
    {"issue": "\"Students should report\" leaves it unclear whether reporting to Public Safety is a duty enforceable under Section 2.2 or merely advisory. Nothing states the consequence of not reporting.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.09}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c012", "issues": []},
  {"clause_id": "c013", "issues": [
    {"issue": "This provision tells a student only that proceedings \"may be initiated\" through the DSRCS under Sections 3.1–3.3 and 7, without identifying the adjudicative process, while the 2013–2014 handbook removes those same sections from Section 19 and assigns them to the Special Examiner's Process in Section 22.6. The provision therefore cannot be squared with the process actually applied to Section 3 charges.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.30}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c014", "issues": [
    {"issue": "This summary states that Section 19.8 \"permits the passive assistance of legal counsel,\" but 19.8 itself says such assistance \"may be allowed,\" i.e., is discretionary and conditioned on consulting SRCS staff. The two descriptions of the same entitlement cannot both be right.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.32}
